<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Annales (Ennius)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Annales was an epic poem in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hexameters by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennius">Quintus Ennius</a>, written in the early second century BCE. In eighteen books it told the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the flight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> to the poet's own day. It was Rome's national epic until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a>, and only about 600 lines survive, in quotations by later writers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem opened, according to its fragments and ancient reports, with a dream in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> appeared to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennius">Ennius</a> and told him that his soul had passed into the poet. The arrangement usually reconstructed groups the books in threes: the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> to Lucius Tarquinius Superbus, the early Republic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhic-war">Pyrrhic War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-seleucid-war">Roman–Seleucid War</a>. Werner Suerbaum and Werner Eck suggest that Ennius first ended with the Aetolian War because of the part one of his patrons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-fulvius-nobilior">Marcus Fulvius Nobilior</a>, played in it.</p><p>Three books added later dealt with the Istrian campaigns of 177 BCE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-wars">Macedonian Wars</a>. Ennius probably drew mostly on Greek records and on the historian Quintus Fabius Pictor. The old view that the poem was modelled on the Annales maximi, the pontiffs' prose annals, is called almost certainly anachronistic by Sander M. Goldberg and Gesine Manuwald.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livius-andronicus">Livius Andronicus</a> and Gnaeus Naevius had used Saturnian verse, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennius">Ennius</a> wrote in dactylic hexameter after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, and the metre became the standard for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> epic. Scholars have described the poem as a mediator between Homer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, though Jackie Elliott argues that much of this picture comes from commentators on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a>, who quoted Ennius to compare him with Virgil. Suetonius reports Virgil saying he was gathering gold from Ennius' muck, and Propertius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> found the poem crude.</p><p>The Annales was studied in schools for centuries, but its popularity waned from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>, and by the end of the 4th century copies were hard to find. No complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Humanists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a>, who made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennius">Ennius</a> a character in his epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, collected the quotations, and the first collection of fragments appeared late in the 16th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> fragments have also been found in the Villa of the Papyri at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herculaneum">Herculaneum</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Most surviving lines come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, Festus, Nonius and Macrobius, and nearly a quarter of them from the first book, which was much quoted in antiquity. Johannes Vahlen led the study of the fragments in the 19th century, and Otto Skutsch's The Annales of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennius">Ennius</a> (1985) is regarded as the standard edition. E. H. Warmington's Loeb Classical Library text of 1935 was superseded by Goldberg and Manuwald's edition of 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Annales (Ennius)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Contact with the Greek world transformed Roman culture: Livius Andronicus translated the Odyssey into Latin around 240 BCE, Plautus and Terence adapted Greek comedy, Ennius wrote the epic <b>Annales</b>, and in the late Republic Cicero, Lucretius, Catullus, Sallust and Caesar created a classical Latin prose and poetry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
